// LoxOps.Stringify (runtime/clr/src/LoxOps.cs) recurses once per level of
// list nesting, with no depth guard at all — unbounded by LoxClosure's own
// FramesMax, because it is plain C# recursion over a value's own shape,
// not a Lox-level call. Printing a list nested this many levels deep
// overflows the CLR's default 8 MiB thread stack partway through
// formatting the resulting StackOverflowException's own message, which
// CoreCLR can then only report as a bare process abort — no Lox.LoxError
// line, no stdout at all (measured with tools/clr_run.sh's LoxHost
// indirection bypassed, running the emitted assembly directly). LoxHost's
// larger stack (tools/clr_run.sh) clears this depth with a wide margin.
//
// This depth also has to stay under native's OWN process-stack ceiling:
// check_clr_probes.sh runs native first and fails this probe by name if
// that run alone fails, and src/object.cpp's stringifyObj has the
// identical unguarded-recursion shape (issue #152). Measured on the
// default 8 MiB process stack, native holds through 25,000 levels and
// segfaults by 27,000, so this file's 20,000 carries about a 1.3x margin
// under native's own ceiling, not a wide one, and that ceiling moves with
// whatever process stack limit the script runs under.
// Both gates that run this probe — check_clr_probes.sh, and the CI step
// that walks notes/translation-probes/ against the CLR backend — raise
// their own soft stack limit before running it, for this reason; a host
// whose HARD limit is already capped below that floor is the one case
// neither raise can cover.
//
// Lives outside the parent translation-probes directory because the two
// gates that walk that directory — check_jvm_probes.sh's own curated
// array, and the CI step that runs tools/diff_runtimes.py over that
// directory's files, non-recursively, against the JVM backend — would
// otherwise both pick it up, and the JVM backend already matches native
// at this same depth (tools/jvm_run.sh's own -Xss64m default clears it
// with room to spare), so moving this file up a level would add nothing
// past what those two gates already cover. This is the opposite of why
// 31_deep_recursion.lox lives here: that probe is moved out because the
// JVM backend DIVERGES from native (no frame-count ceiling of its own);
// this one is moved out because the JVM backend already MATCHES native.
// check_clr_probes.sh names this file directly, so the CLR checkpoint
// still runs it.
var x = 0;
var n = 0;
while (n < 20000) {
    x = [x];
    n = n + 1;
}
print x;
